const faq = [
  { q: 'Are there ravens in Greenland?', a: 'Yes — the common raven (Corvus corax varius in some classifications) is a year-round resident of Greenland, one of the very few passerine birds to overwinter in the country\'s extreme arctic and subarctic climate. Ravens occur along Greenland\'s west coast from the southern tip to well above the Arctic Circle, and are found on the east coast as well. They are most abundant in areas near human settlements — particularly around the dog-sledding communities and food-processing sites that provide reliable food supplementation in winter.' },
  { q: 'How do ravens survive Greenland winters?', a: 'Greenland ravens survive the polar winter through several key adaptations: communal roosting in sheltered cliffs and buildings (body heat sharing reduces energy expenditure); association with human settlements (traditional Greenlandic communities and modern towns both provide food waste); exploitation of marine mammals — raven presence at polar bear kills and seal carcasses on the sea ice is documented; cache recovery (recovering fat-rich food cached in autumn); and extreme cold tolerance from feather insulation and metabolic adaptation. At the darkest period (winter solstice), Greenland ravens may have only a few hours of twilight, requiring fat-efficient roosting and minimal activity.' },
  { q: 'What is the raven called in Greenlandic (Kalaallisut)?', a: 'The raven is called tulugaq in Kalaallisut (Greenlandic), the Inuit language spoken in Greenland. This word is cognate with the Inuktitut tulugaq (raven) used across the Canadian Arctic and closely related to words for raven across all Inuit-Yupik languages — reflecting the raven\'s consistent presence and cultural significance across the entire circumpolar Inuit world. The raven appears in Greenlandic stories and oral tradition as a creator figure, a trickster, and a companion of hunters.' },
];
